{"name":"Reclaimed Wood","hex":"#bab6ab","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"reclaimed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having been acquired by the process of reclaiming."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of reclaim"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reclaimed"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reclaimed lumber","blocks":[{"text":"Reclaimed lumber is processed wood retrieved from its original application for purposes of subsequent use. Most reclaimed lumber comes from timbers and decking rescued from old barns, factories and warehouses, although some companies use wood from less traditional structures such as boxcars, coal mines and wine barrels. Reclaimed or antique lumber is used primarily for decoration and home building, for example for siding, architectural details, cabinetry, furniture and flooring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Uhuru_Design_Cyclone_Lounger.png/330px-Uhuru_Design_Cyclone_Lounger.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reclaimed_lumber","wikidata":"Q7302557"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/The_Oaks_Interior.jpg/500px-The_Oaks_Interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/The_Oaks_Interior.jpg/1280px-The_Oaks_Interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/The_Oaks_Interior.jpg/500px-The_Oaks_Interior.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This dining hall uses wood recycled from barns for flooring, walls, and furniture.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Oaks_Interior.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg/500px-The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg/1280px-The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg/500px-The_Employment_of_Women_on_the_Western_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q6769.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Tarrant hut during World War I built with wood from old boxes and crates","link":"https://en.wikipe